index 0000000..107f00d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/protocol.md
@@ -0,0 +1,74 @@
+# The ingest protocol
+
+`lakelet` accepts telemetry over TCP, as frames of bytes. This page is the contract: what a client
+writes, what the daemon refuses, and why the two are the same on every machine.
+
+The decoder lives in [`src/protocol/frame.rs`](../src/protocol/frame.rs) and the record format in
+[`src/protocol/batch.rs`](../src/protocol/batch.rs). If this page and those files disagree, the files
+are right and this page is a bug.
+
+## A frame
+
+```text
+ 0       4       5                    5+n      9+n
+ +-------+-------+---------------------+--------+
+ | length|version| payload (n bytes)   | crc32  |
+ +-------+-------+---------------------+--------+
+  u32 LE   u8         n bytes           u32 LE
+```
+
+- **length** is the payload's length in bytes — not the frame's. A reader can decide how much more to
+  read from those four bytes alone, before it has any payload at all.
+- **version** is `1`. It sits here, in front of the payload, so a build that does not speak a
+  version refuses the frame as soon as the fifth byte arrives, instead of buffering a payload it
+  cannot parse.
+- **payload** is opaque to the framing layer.
+- **crc32** covers the payload only. A corrupted length therefore cannot make the checksum pass by
+  accident: the length is what decides where the payload ends, and the checksum then disagrees.
+
+Nine bytes of framing, and the largest payload is `MAX_PAYLOAD` (1 MiB). A length above that is
+refused *before* any buffer is reserved for it: the limit exists so that a peer cannot choose how much
+memory this process holds.
+
+## A payload
+
+```text
+ 0..4   count: u32 LE
+ then, per record:
+   u16 LE  name length
+   bytes   series name (UTF-8, e.g. "cpu,host=h0")
+   i64 LE  timestamp, milliseconds since the Unix epoch
+   f64 LE  value
+```
+
+A batch is all of it or none of it. A payload whose count promises more records than the bytes carry,
+a record that ends in the middle of its value, a series name that is not UTF-8, an empty series name,
+and bytes left over after the last record are all refused with a sentence saying which one it was.
+`lakelet/4.7-columnar-segments` replaces this layout *inside the store*; the wire keeps this one,
+because a wire format nobody can debug by hand is a wire format nobody debugs at 3am.
+
+## What a peer can do to us
+
+The decoder answers three ways, and the difference matters to whoever writes the next client:
+
+| What arrived | The answer | Why |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| fewer bytes than a frame | `Incomplete { needed }` | `needed` is how many more will be enough — read that many, not one byte more |
+| a length above 1 MiB | `Invalid` | refused before the payload is reserved |
+| a version that is not 1 | `Invalid` | refused from the header alone |
+| a payload whose checksum does not match | `Invalid` | the sentence names both numbers |
+| a whole frame | `Frame(frame, consumed)` | `consumed` may be less than the buffer: two frames can arrive in one read |
+
+A refusal ends the connection. After bytes that are not a frame, nothing that follows can be trusted
+to start at a boundary, so the daemon counts the frame, logs the reason, and closes — one client's
+garbage is not the process's problem.
+
+## What is not here yet
+
+- no compression: `lakelet/4.7-columnar-segments` is where bytes get smaller;
+- no acknowledgement: a client knows a frame was stored because the daemon answered `accepted` on its
+  connection, not because the frame carried an id — per-batch acknowledgement is
+  `lakelet/4.5-batch-writes`' subject, where batching makes it worth the round trip;
+- no backpressure: a client that sends faster than the store can write is
+  `lakelet/4.6-ingest-backpressure`'s subject;
+- version negotiation: a version this build does not speak is refused, not downgraded.
